## Test Coverage Summary (for review purposes)

The integration test validates all 12 acceptance criteria from
`docs/specs/orchestrator-recovery-after-mixed-completion.md`:

- AC #1  — terminal Merge PR step (step 9) flips `feature.status = "complete"`
           and bumps `currentStep`; non-terminal steps do not.
- AC #2  — a mixed sprint (one complete, one escalated) finalizes as `escalated`,
           never left `in-progress` (via `deriveSprintStatus`).
- AC #3  — all-complete sprint finalizes `complete`; eligible for shared steps 10–13.
- AC #4  — `resume_sprint` exposes an additive, optional `feature` selector;
           legacy calls without it remain valid.
- AC #5  — implicit single-target resume when exactly one feature is escalated.
- AC #6  — explicit multi-target resume: >1 escalated errors and lists slugs;
           a valid slug targets that feature.
- AC #7  — per-feature step reset (`attempts=0`, `failures=[]`, `status=pending`),
           feature→`in-progress`, sprint→`in-progress`, re-entry at the escalated
           step, feedback injected.
- AC #8  — completed sibling is byte-for-byte untouched by the resume.
- AC #9  — re-escalation supported with a fresh 3-attempt budget, no cap; a
           successful re-attempt finalizes an all-complete sprint.
- AC #11 — `renderProgressTable` names the escalated feature and its step.
- AC #12 — the escalated step lives under `state.features[i].steps`, not the
           top-level `state.steps`; mixed sprints are resumable (no stale
           "cannot be resumed" / "no escalated step found").

Plus edge cases: all-features-escalated, genuinely-in-progress refusal,
invalid/non-escalated `feature` slugs, `approve`-on-escalated no-op redirect,
and the unchanged single-feature (`state.features` null) escalated-resume path.

Companion BDD scenarios: `tests/bdd/orchestrator-recovery-after-mixed-completion.feature`.
